.nh
.TH "PVEFORGE" "1" "Sep 2026" "pveforge" "pveforge manual"

.SH NAME
pveforge-vm-snapshot-delete - Delete one or more of a VM's snapshots, newest first


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBpveforge vm snapshot delete <target-id> <vmid> <name>\&... [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Delete one or more of a VM's snapshots. The names are deleted newest first,
whatever order they are given in (read from the snapshot list under the
VM's lock), one at a time, each confirmed gone afterwards. A name that is
not a snapshot of the VM is reported as absent, not refused. If a delete
fails, the command stops and says which were already deleted; running the
same command again finishes the job.

.PP
To clear the way for a rollback, pass the snapshots vm snapshot rollback
names as newer than its target.

.PP
Locking: the VM's pveforge lock is held for the whole command (a shared
read lock for list). It serialises pveforge processes only: a change made
at the same time from PVE's web UI or qm is not held off.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for delete

.PP
\fB--lock-wait\fP=0s
	how long to wait for another pveforge process's lock on this object before giving up (0 = the 15m0s default; at most 1h0m0s). The default exceeds the 10m0s ceiling on waiting for a PVE task because a legitimate lock holder may be in such a wait the whole time

.PP
\fB-o\fP, \fB--output\fP="kv"
	output format: "kv" or "json"

.PP
\fB--roster\fP=""
	path to the roster file (overrides PVEFORGE_ROSTER and the default ./pveforge.toml)


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBpveforge-vm-snapshot(1)\fP
